use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

// NOTE on the operations request (log file + metadata-service fetch):
// neither is implemented, deliberately. This node's `with` clause grants
// exactly `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`. Writing `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`
// would need a filesystem capability and fetching from
// `metadata.internal.example` would need `aap:caps/http-client`; neither is
// granted, so this world does not import them. Granting them is a change to
// the graph's `with` clause, not to this body.

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        // Only mark items seen once the digest actually went out; otherwise
        // the next run should consider them again.
        if published.published {
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                if id.is_empty() {
                    continue;
                }
                state_rw::put(id, &published.date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        // `recorded` is a u32, so `recorded >= 0` holds by construction.
        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I did not include the two operations additions: the log-file append and the metadata-service fetch both need authority this node isn't granted. Its `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, so there is no filesystem or `http-client` import to call. Adding them means changing the node's `with` clause in the graph first.

One behaviour to confirm: the node marks the considered ids as seen only when `published` is true, and returns `recorded: 0` otherwise. I chose that so a failed publish doesn't cause items to be skipped on the next run.

